# The ship.
#
# 16 x 16 texels at `art::TEXELS_PER_UNIT` = 10 is 1.6 x 1.6 world units, drawn
# against a kill radius of `game::SHIP_RADIUS` = 0.55 — so the hull is visibly
# **larger** than the sphere that kills it. That is the right way round and it
# is what `game.rs` asks for in as many words: "a ship whose kill radius matches
# its longest spine dies to near misses, and asteroids is a game of near
# misses". The fins and the nozzle are the part that sticks out past the sphere.
#
# **The nose points up the frame**, because a `.crpix` row 0 is the world's +Y
# and `game::heading_vector` puts a heading of zero along +Y. That makes the
# sprite's rotation the ship's heading with no offset — see `art::ship_sprite`,
# which passes it straight through.
crpix: 16 16 1 5 1

palette:
  . c None
  k c #161a26
  w c #dfe9f5
  b c #4bb3e8
  r c #ff9a3c

frame hull:
  .......kk.......
  .......kk.......
  ......kwwk......
  ......kwwk......
  .....kwbbwk.....
  .....kwbbwk.....
  ....kwwbbwwk....
  ....kwwbbwwk....
  ...kwwwwwwwwk...
  ...kwwwwwwwwk...
  ..kwwwwwwwwwwk..
  ..kwwkwwwwkwwk..
  .kwwk.kwwk.kwwk.
  .kwk..krrk..kwk.
  .kk...krrk...kk.
  ......kkkk......

sample: pixel
